  "published": "2026-11-20T09:33:40.807Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "_New_\n- Files with multiple tagging systems : ID3v2 fields are depriorized in favour of native tagging when the ID3v2 parser raises warnings\n- MP4/M4A : `GENRE` field ID is now properly mapped to `Track.Genre`/`IMetaData.Genre` instead of ending up in `AdditionalFields`\n_Changed_\n- MP4 /M4A : Detect more details about the underlying codec\n- FLAC : Is now VBR (as it technically uses variable bitrate)\n- `Track.MetadataFormats` : \"Vorbis (OGG)\" and \"Vorbis (FLAC)\" are not differentiated anymore (both are now displayed as \"Vorbis\")\n- `Format.ID` is now deprecated. Use one of the following three flavours :\n- `Format.MainID` : stable ID across all releases to identify a given format (e.g. `MetaDataIOFactory.TagType.ID3V2` for ID3v2)\n- `Format.SubID` : stable complement to `Format.MainID` across all releases to recognize a given sub-format (e.g. `3` for ID3v2.3)\n- `Format.UniqueID` : ID guaranteed to be unique, but not necessarily stable across all releases\n- `AudioFormat.Name` formatting has evolved to \"Format Name\", \"Format Name / Subformat name\" or \"Container Name / Codec Name\"\n- Might break things on your side if you've been relying on that textual field instead of an ID\n- If you need to access to the Container or Format name only, you may call `AudioFormat.ContainerName`\n- If you need to access to the Codec or Subformat name only, you may call `AudioFormat.DataFormat.Name`\nThanks to @hsahgal for his multiple contributions to the codebase 🚀\n***Binaries are available from nuGet***",
